# Draft Bill  
## Public Authentication and Registry Reform Act

### Explanatory Note

This Bill replaces the exclusive statutory authentication function presently assigned to a closed association of licensed notaries with an open, government-operated public registry. It preserves reliable authentication, protects personal data, maintains appropriate safeguards against fraud, and permits qualified persons and entities to submit documents directly without mandatory use of a particular professional association.

The Bill provides for:

- repeal of the association’s exclusive authentication monopoly;
- establishment of a public authentication registry;
- direct electronic and in-person submission;
- uniform, transparent fees;
- identity verification and audit requirements;
- recognition of qualified private authentication providers on a non-exclusive basis;
- appeal, correction, and review procedures;
- transitional arrangements for existing records and pending applications; and
- oversight, security, accessibility, and privacy protections.

---

## Be it enacted by the Legislature as follows:

### 1. Short title

This Act may be cited as the **Public Authentication and Registry Reform Act**.

### 2. Purposes

The purposes of this Act are to:

1. ensure that official documents may be authenticated through an open, accessible, and non-exclusive public system;
2. eliminate unnecessary statutory barriers to direct public access;
3. maintain confidence in the integrity and provenance of official records;
4. protect personal information and confidential material;
5. promote interoperability, competition, innovation, and reasonable fees; and
6. provide transparent review and accountability mechanisms.

### 3. Definitions

In this Act:

**“Authentication”** means the official verification of the origin, integrity, status, or execution of a document, as specified by regulation.

**“Authority”** means the [Registrar-General/Public Records Authority] designated under section 6.

**“Document”** includes a paper or electronic instrument, certificate, record, copy, extract, or other item prescribed by regulation.

**“Official document”** means a document issued, certified, maintained, or otherwise recognized by a public body or by a person authorized by law.

**“Registry”** means the Public Authentication Registry established under section 5.

**“Submitter”** means a person or entity seeking authentication, registration, verification, or issuance of a registry record.

### 4. Repeal of exclusive authentication right

1. The provision of [identify enactment and section] that reserves authentication of [identify class of documents] exclusively to [name of association] is repealed.

2. No person or organization shall be required, as a condition of validity, enforceability, filing, acceptance, or public recognition of a document, to obtain authentication from [name of association] or from any other exclusive professional body, except as expressly provided by this Act or another Act of Parliament.

3. Nothing in this section prevents a licensed notary, lawyer, accountant, records professional, or other qualified person from providing authentication services on a non-exclusive basis, subject to applicable law.

4. Any agreement, rule, practice, or administrative direction that purports to preserve the repealed exclusivity is void to the extent of the inconsistency.

### 5. Establishment of Public Authentication Registry

1. The Authority shall establish and maintain a Public Authentication Registry.

2. The Registry shall provide, in electronic and, where reasonably necessary, physical form:

   a. submission of documents for authentication or registration;  
   b. verification of the identity and authority of the issuer and submitter;  
   c. assignment of a unique registry identifier;  
   d. recording of the date, time, source, status, and material attributes of an authentication;  
   e. issuance of a certificate, seal, electronic credential, or other prescribed evidence of authentication;  
   f. public verification of records designated as public under this Act; and  
   g. correction, withdrawal, suspension, and historical-status functions.

3. The Registry shall not be construed as determining the substantive legal validity of a document unless another enactment expressly provides otherwise.

4. The Authority shall design the Registry to support recognized electronic-signature, digital-identity, accessibility, and interoperability standards.

### 6. Designation and duties of Authority

1. The Minister shall, by notice published in the Gazette, designate a public body as the Authority.

2. The Authority shall:

   a. operate and secure the Registry;  
   b. publish procedures, technical standards, service levels, and fees;  
   c. maintain an auditable record of all authentication events;  
   d. provide reasonable access to persons with disabilities and persons lacking reliable internet access;  
   e. establish identity-verification and fraud-prevention controls;  
   f. train and supervise Registry personnel;  
   g. publish annual performance and transparency reports; and  
   h. consult affected stakeholders before making material changes to Registry rules.

3. The Authority shall act impartially and shall not favor any professional association, service provider, or category of submitter.

### 7. Access and submission

1. Any person or entity may submit a document to the Registry if that person or entity:

   a. has a lawful interest in the document;  
   b. satisfies applicable identity and authority requirements; and  
   c. pays the prescribed fee, if any.

2. The Authority shall not require use of a notary, association member, intermediary, or other professional unless a specific statutory safeguard requires it.

3. The Authority may require additional evidence where reasonably necessary to:

   a. establish identity or authority;  
   b. detect fraud, coercion, forgery, or duplication;  
   c. protect a vulnerable person;  
   d. protect confidential or legally privileged information; or  
   e. comply with a court order or another enactment.

4. A refusal to accept a submission must be provided in writing, with reasons and information about review rights.

### 8. Legal effect of Registry authentication

1. A Registry authentication is prima facie evidence of the matters expressly recorded in the authentication.

2. Unless another law provides otherwise, a document shall not be rejected solely because it was authenticated through the Registry rather than by a member of [name of former association].

3. Authentication does not:

   a. establish that the contents of a document are true;  
   b. cure fraud, illegality, incapacity, lack of authority, or want of consideration; or  
   c. prevent a court or competent authority from examining the document’s validity.

4. A court or public body may require further evidence where the circumstances reasonably warrant it.

### 9. Recognition of qualified private providers

1. The Authority may accredit private persons or entities to provide authentication-related services on a non-exclusive basis.

2. Accreditation criteria shall be objective, publicly available, and proportionate to the risks involved, and may include:

   a. professional competence;  
   b. identity-verification capability;  
   c. information-security controls;  
   d. insurance or financial responsibility;  
   e. record-retention capacity; and  
   f. compliance with privacy and anti-fraud requirements.

3. An accredited provider shall not represent that it has an exclusive or government-granted right to authenticate documents.

4. The Authority may suspend or revoke accreditation for fraud, material non-compliance, repeated negligence, or other prescribed grounds, subject to notice and review.

### 10. Fees

1. Fees charged by the Authority shall be:

   a. prescribed by regulation;  
   b. published in advance;  
   c. reasonable and related to the cost of providing the service; and  
   d. subject to waiver or reduction where prescribed.

2. The Authority shall publish an annual statement of fees collected and the costs of operating the Registry.

3. No fee may be charged for public verification of a record where the record is designated for unrestricted public access, unless prescribed for reasonable cost recovery.

### 11. Privacy and confidentiality

1. The Authority shall collect, use, disclose, retain, and destroy personal information only as authorized by law and as reasonably necessary for the purposes of this Act.

2. The Authority shall implement:

   a. data minimization;  
   b. role-based access controls;  
   c. encryption in transit and at rest;  
   d. audit logging;  
   e. breach detection and response;  
   f. retention and disposal schedules; and  
   g. procedures for correction of inaccurate personal information.

3. A record shall be publicly searchable only to the extent that:

   a. another law requires public access;  
   b. the submitter has validly requested public access; or  
   c. the Authority determines, under published criteria, that public access is necessary to serve a compelling public interest.

4. Sensitive personal information, protected addresses, signatures, identity documents, and other prescribed information shall not be publicly displayed except as expressly authorized by law.

5. The Authority shall conduct and publish a privacy impact assessment before the Registry begins operation and whenever a material expansion of data collection or public access is proposed.

### 12. Security and integrity

1. The Authority shall maintain technical and administrative safeguards reasonably designed to ensure that Registry records are:

   a. attributable to the recorded source;  
   b. protected against unauthorized alteration or deletion;  
   c. recoverable after loss or disruption; and  
   d. auditable throughout their retention period.

2. Every material alteration, correction, suspension, or withdrawal shall preserve an immutable historical audit trail.

3. The Authority shall promptly investigate suspected compromise and shall notify affected persons and the appropriate oversight body where required by law.

4. Regulations may prescribe standards for electronic seals, signatures, timestamps, cryptographic verification, disaster recovery, and independent security testing.

### 13. Corrections, suspensions, and withdrawals

1. A person with a lawful interest may apply to correct an inaccurate Registry entry.

2. The Authority may suspend or mark a record where there are reasonable grounds to suspect:

   a. forgery or fraud;  
   b. unauthorized submission;  
   c. material error;  
   d. duplicate registration; or  
   e. a court order or statutory prohibition.

3. No correction, suspension, or withdrawal shall erase the historical fact that a prior entry existed.

4. Except where immediate action is necessary to prevent harm or fraud, the Authority shall provide notice and a reasonable opportunity to respond before taking adverse action.

### 14. Review and appeal

1. A person affected by a decision under this Act may request internal review within [30] days after receiving notice.

2. The reviewing officer shall be independent of the original decision-maker where practicable.

3. A decision on internal review shall include reasons and notice of any further appeal right.

4. A person may appeal a final decision to [the Administrative Appeals Tribunal/court] within [30] days, subject to applicable procedural rules.

5. Filing an appeal does not automatically suspend a decision unless the reviewing body orders otherwise.

### 15. Offences and penalties

1. A person commits an offence if that person knowingly:

   a. submits a forged or materially altered document;  
   b. impersonates an issuer, submitter, registrar, or accredited provider;  
   c. interferes with the Registry;  
   d. accesses protected information without authorization; or  
   e. falsely represents that authentication is exclusive to a particular association.

2. An offence under subsection (1) is punishable by:

   a. for an individual, a fine not exceeding [amount] or imprisonment not exceeding [term], or both; and  
   b. for a body corporate, a fine not exceeding [amount].

3. A person does not commit an offence merely because a submitted document is later found to be inaccurate, provided the person acted honestly and with reasonable care.

### 16. Transitional arrangements

1. An authentication validly issued before the commencement of this Act remains valid unless set aside under another law.

2. Existing records held by [name of former association] that are required to establish legal rights, status, or historical provenance shall be transferred or made accessible to the Authority under an approved migration plan.

3. The former association shall, within [90] days after receiving a lawful request from the Authority:

   a. provide records in a usable and secure format;  
   b. provide metadata necessary to interpret those records;  
   c. identify unresolved, suspended, or disputed entries; and  
   d. cooperate in verification and reconciliation.

4. The Authority shall publish the migration plan, including safeguards for confidentiality, integrity, chain of custody, and public access.

5. Pending applications may be completed under the former procedure for a period not exceeding [six months] after commencement, unless the applicant elects to use the Registry.

6. Nothing in this section requires the transfer of records that are protected from disclosure by legal privilege, a court order, or another law, except to the extent authorized by that law.

### 17. Treatment of former association

1. [Name of former association] may continue to operate as a voluntary professional organization, subject to applicable law.

2. It shall not:

   a. claim an exclusive statutory right repealed by this Act;  
   b. obstruct access to records that the Authority is legally entitled to receive; or  
   c. impose a condition of membership on access to the Registry.

3. Regulations may provide for compensation for reasonable, documented costs of record migration, but no compensation is payable for the loss of a monopoly or anticipated future profits unless another law expressly provides otherwise.

### 18. Regulations

The Minister may make regulations concerning:

1. classes of documents subject to authentication;
2. identity and authority verification;
3. electronic and physical submission procedures;
4. technical, accessibility, and interoperability standards;
5. fees, exemptions, and refunds;
6. privacy, retention, and public-access rules;
7. accreditation of private providers;
8. audit, reporting, and security requirements;
9. forms, notices, and certificates; and
10. transitional and incidental matters necessary to give effect to this Act.

### 19. Independent oversight

1. Within [three years] after commencement, and at least every [five years] thereafter, the [Auditor-General/Information Commissioner/independent reviewer] shall review the operation of the Registry.

2. The review shall consider:

   a. accessibility and cost;  
   b. security and privacy;  
   c. processing times and error rates;  
   d. fraud and misuse;  
   e. effects on competition and professional services;  
   f. the effectiveness of appeals and corrections; and  
   g. whether legislative amendments are required.

3. The reviewer shall table a report before the Legislature.

### 20. Consequential amendments

The enactments listed in Schedule 1 are amended as set out in that Schedule.

### 21. Commencement

1. Sections 1, 2, 3, 18, 20, and this section come into force on Royal Assent.

2. The remaining provisions come into force on a day appointed by proclamation.

3. Different days may be appointed for different provisions or purposes.

