**MOTION TO REVOKE PERSONAL STATUTORY EXEMPTION [Reference No. / Date]**

**Moved by:** [Your name], Councillor
**Seconded by:** [Name of seconder]

**WHEREAS:**

1. The Council is empowered, under [cite the provision of the district's governing act or code that authorizes the council to review, rescind, or recommend the rescission of personal statutory exemptions from building-and-land regulations — e.g., "Section __ of the [District] Enabling Act, [year]"], to consider the revocation of personal exemptions previously granted within its jurisdiction;

2. By [statute / ordinance / order] numbered [____], enacted [date], a personal statutory exemption from the [building regulations / land-use regulations / subdivision regulations] was granted to [name of resident], in respect of [parcel identifier or address], thereby removing the said property from the application of [cite the specific regulations from which the exemption was granted];

3. The said exemption has remained in force for approximately [number] years, during which time the regulatory landscape, planning framework, and circumstances of the district have materially changed, including [list changed circumstances — e.g., adoption of a new district plan, population growth, infrastructure changes, environmental considerations, etc.];

4. The continued operation of the exemption is inconsistent with the present [district plan / zoning scheme / building code / land-use policy] and produces an inequitable regulatory outcome, in that [name of resident] alone, of all persons on the register, is not subject to requirements binding on every other property owner in the district;

5. It is in the public interest that all properties within the district be subject to a uniform and contemporary regulatory regime, save where general exemptions of broad application are warranted;

**NOW THEREFORE BE IT RESOLVED** that the Council:

(a) **Records** its finding that the personal statutory exemption granted to [name of resident] by [citation], in respect of [property], is no longer in the public interest and ought to be revoked;

(b) **Directs** the [Clerk / Chief Executive / designated officer] to:
   (i) prepare a report setting out the legal basis for revocation, the procedural steps required under [cite applicable statute], and the implications for the affected property;
   (ii) cause notice of this motion and of the proposed revocation to be served upon [name of resident] at [address], in accordance with [cite notice provisions];
   (iii) cause public notice of the proposed revocation to be given in the manner required by [cite publication requirements];

(c) **Schedules** a public hearing on the proposed revocation to be held at the Council's [next regular meeting / special meeting to be convened for this purpose], at which [name of resident] and any other affected party shall be afforded a reasonable opportunity to be heard;

(d) **Resolves** that, following the hearing, the Council will determine whether to:
   (i) revoke the exemption in whole;
   (ii) revoke the exemption in part;
   (iii) modify the conditions attached to the exemption; or
   (iv) decline to revoke the exemption;

(e) **Authorizes** the [Chair / Chief Executive / Clerk] to take all steps necessary to give effect to this motion, including the transmission of the Council's determination to [the granting authority / the relevant minister / the registrar, as required by the statute under which the exemption was originally granted].

**FURTHER RESOLVED** that this motion shall take effect upon its adoption by the Council, but that any revocation of the exemption itself shall not take effect until the procedural steps set out above have been completed and the Council has made a final determination following the hearing.

---

**Procedural notes for your consideration:**

- **Authority to revoke.** In many jurisdictions a personal statutory exemption granted by a higher legislative body cannot be revoked by a district council acting alone; the council's role may be limited to recommending revocation or triggering a review. Confirm the precise scope of your council's power before relying on clause (e) above.
- **Notice and hearing.** Personal exemptions are typically treated as a form of vested right. Even where the statute does not expressly require a hearing, natural justice and most enabling acts will require notice to the affected party and an opportunity to be heard. The motion above builds that in.
- **Grounds.** Councils that have successfully revoked long-standing exemptions have generally done so on the basis of materially changed circumstances or demonstrated inconsistency with current planning policy, rather than on the basis of the identity of the holder. Centring the motion on changed circumstances (recital 3) and regulatory inconsistency (recital 4) will make it more defensible than a motion framed around the individual.
- **Record.** Ensure the Clerk places a complete record of the motion, the hearing, and the council's determination on file, as this is likely to be relevant if the matter is challenged.

